## 2. The fallback rule

Detection fails. A capability query returns nothing, a preference is unreadable, an environment
reports something implausible. What happens then is a design decision, and leaving it implicit means
it was made by whichever code path ran first.

<!-- DECIDE: the project-wide default. The defensible rule is to fall back to the **most capable
     path that is safe for everyone** rather than the most featureful: when motion preference is
     unknown, reduce motion; when input method is unknown, support both touch targets and keyboard
     traversal. That costs a little polish in the unknown case and avoids excluding someone in it.
     If this project takes the opposite position anywhere, name where and why. -->

## 3. What never varies

<!-- DECIDE: the invariants that hold regardless of capability. Usually: nothing is reachable in one
     environment only, accessibility minimums do not relax, no content is removed rather than
     re-presented, and no adaptation hides a path with no alternative.

     The failure this section prevents is specific: an adaptation that begins as "present this
     differently here" and becomes "omit it here", at which point some users can no longer do
     something at all — a product decision made in a rendering branch. -->

